# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ancre de version
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ne contient aucun code. Son unique fonction est de rendre impossible toute
# derive de version entre conteneurs : chaque role en depend a l'egalite
# stricte, donc apt refuse tout melange de trains de livraison.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Package: drumee-release
Architecture: all
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: Drumee release train anchor
 Declares the release train that every Drumee role package belongs to.
 This package ships no runtime code: it installs only
 /usr/share/drumee/release, a plain key/value file recording the release
 version, the APT channel it was published to, and the build timestamp.
 .
 Every drumee-role-* package depends on this exact version, which makes it
 impossible for two containers of the same deployment to run components
 from different releases.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Exclusion mutuelle des roles
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chaque role fournit ET entre en conflit avec le paquet virtuel
# "drumee-role". Un paquet n'entrant jamais en conflit avec lui-meme, l'effet
# net est qu'un seul role peut etre installe a la fois : le monolithe ne peut
# pas etre reconstitue par accident dans une image.
#
# Si une variante tout-en-un devient necessaire un jour, elle prend la forme
# d'un paquet drumee-role-aio qui fournit le meme paquet virtuel et depend
# directement des composants.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Package: drumee-role-app
# "all" bien que ce role ne soit satisfaisable que la ou drumee-server-pod existe.
# Depuis 2.9.98 ce dernier est Architecture: any et seul amd64 est construit (voir
# docs/distribution.md §9.1), donc sur arm64 ce role est refuse par apt avec
# "drumee-server-pod ... but it is not installable" — echec explicite, et non une
# image qui demarre puis casse au premier require(). Ne pas annoncer arm64 dans la
# matrice des images de ce role avant qu'un server-pod arm64 soit publie.
Architecture: all
Section: web
Provides: drumee-role
Conflicts: drumee-role
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         drumee-release (= ${binary:Version}),
         drumee-server-pod (= ${drumee:ServerVersion}),
         drumee-bootstrap (= ${drumee:BootstrapVersion}),
         drumee-node-runtime (= ${drumee:NodeRuntimeVersion}),
         nodejs (>= 22),
         nodejs (<< 23),
         mariadb-client,
         redis-tools
Description: Drumee application role (REST and push services)
 Runtime role for the container serving the Drumee backend: the REST API on
 port 24000 and the page/WebSocket server on port 23000, both supervised by
 pm2 in fork mode.
 .
 The pm2 supervisor and the pinned set of global Node modules are supplied
 by drumee-node-runtime rather than by an unpinned "npm install -g", so the
 JavaScript dependency set is versioned like everything else.

Package: drumee-role-web
Architecture: all
Section: httpd
Provides: drumee-role
Conflicts: drumee-role
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         drumee-release (= ${binary:Version}),
         drumee-ui-pod (= ${drumee:UiVersion}),
         drumee-static (= ${drumee:StaticVersion}),
         drumee-bootstrap (= ${drumee:BootstrapVersion}),
         nginx,
         libnginx-mod-stream
Description: Drumee web role (reverse proxy and static assets)
 Runtime role for the container terminating HTTP, serving the compiled LETC
 frontend bundles and the static asset tree, and proxying dynamic requests
 to the application role.
 .
 No build step happens here: drumee-ui-pod already contains the webpack
 output, produced once when the package was built in CI.

Package: drumee-role-converter
Architecture: all
Section: video
Provides: drumee-role
Conflicts: drumee-role
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         drumee-release (= ${binary:Version}),
         drumee-bootstrap (= ${drumee:BootstrapVersion}),
         drumee-node-runtime (= ${drumee:NodeRuntimeVersion}),
         nodejs (>= 22),
         nodejs (<< 23),
         libreoffice-core,
         libreoffice-writer,
         libreoffice-calc,
         libreoffice-impress,
         fonts-liberation,
         poppler-utils,
         graphicsmagick,
         ghostscript,
         ffmpeg,
         7zip
Description: Drumee converter role (file format conversion)
 Runtime role for the PURE converters: office document rendering, PDF text
 extraction and rasterising, image resizing, video and audio transcoding,
 archive creation and extraction. File in, file out.
 .
 Named for what it does rather than for the subsystem it came from. The
 offline/media tree in drumee-server-pod mixes two different jobs: format
 conversion, which needs nothing but the file, and CONTENT operations
 (add, download, purge, transfer, backfill-posters) which need the database
 and the MFS model. Only the first kind belongs here — that is what makes
 the isolation below true rather than aspirational.
 .
 It carries the heaviest dependencies in the platform and is the only
 component that parses untrusted user documents, so it runs with no
 database credentials and no inbound network exposure. Progress and results
 travel out over the Redis live-update channel, which the platform already
 uses to reach the browser: the WebSocket sessions belong to the app role
 and a short-lived worker could not hold them anyway.
 .
 Every dependency here is a binary something actually shells out to, measured
 against server-team and against the wrapper scripts in /usr/share/drumee/bin
 rather than assumed:
 .
   libreoffice-*   soffice, 41 call sites — the bulk of the conversion work
   fonts-liberation  soffice substitutes these for the Microsoft core fonts;
                   without them page breaks move and the PDF stops matching
   poppler-utils   pdftotext (4), pdfinfo (3)
   graphicsmagick  gm (3) — and note that every bare "convert" and "mogrify"
                   in the source is "gm convert" / "gm mogrify", so no
                   ImageMagick and no graphicsmagick-imagemagick-compat
   ghostscript     NOT optional, and the reason it is listed explicitly is a
                   trap this role walked into. create-doc-preview.sh rasterises
                   the first PDF page with "gm convert -density 200 file.pdf[0]",
                   which gm performs by shelling out to gs. graphicsmagick only
                   RECOMMENDS ghostscript, and every apt-get here runs with
                   --no-install-recommends; measured on Trixie, that
                   combination fails with "Postscript delegate failed" and no
                   preview. The native channel never noticed because it installs
                   the libreoffice METAPACKAGE, which depends on ghostscript —
                   so taking only the modules we use silently removed it
   ffmpeg          ffmpeg (8), ffprobe (2)
   7zip            7z (16). Not p7zip-full: that is a transitional package on
                   Trixie, and 7zip itself owns /usr/bin/7z
 .
 Only the LibreOffice modules used for conversion are pulled in, not the
 libreoffice metapackage. dcraw was dropped: nothing in the platform invokes
 it, so it was carrying a RAW decoder no code path could reach.

Package: drumee-role-dns
Architecture: all
Section: net
Provides: drumee-role
Conflicts: drumee-role
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         drumee-release (= ${binary:Version}),
         drumee-bootstrap (= ${drumee:BootstrapVersion}),
         bind9,
         bind9-utils,
         bind9-dnsutils
Description: Drumee DNS role (authoritative zone server)
 Runtime role for the container answering as the authoritative name server
 for the deployment's zone, including the TSIG-signed dynamic updates used
 to answer ACME DNS-01 challenges for wildcard certificates.
 .
 Only required when the deployment answers challenges itself rather than
 through a provider API or a DNS-capable TLS terminator.

